      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Obesity, a critical component of metabolic syndrome (MetS), is associated with depression. Deficiency of brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) is involved in the mechanism of depression. We hypothesized that weight reduction would improve depressive symptoms via increasing BDNF levels in obese men."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Male adults with obesity were enrolled in a weight-reduction program for twelve weeks. All subjects underwent daily caloric restriction and an exercise program which was regularly assessed in group classes. Fasting blood samples and Zung Self-Rating Depression Scale (Zung SDS) scores were collected for assessments before and after the study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A total of 36 subjects completed this program. The average reduction in body weight was 8.4\u2009±\u20095.1 kg (8.8\u2009±\u20095.1%, P\u2009<\u20090.001). Fasting serum BDNF significantly increased after the study (from 40.4\u2009±\u20097.8 to 46.9\u2009±\u20098.9 ng/ml, P\u2009<\u20090.001). However, the depression symptoms, as assessed by the Zung Self-Rating Depression Scale (Zung SDS), did not reduce significantly (P\u2009=\u20090.486). Divided into subgroups based on changes in BDNF, Zung SDS scores were significantly reduced in subjects with greater BDNF increase than in those with minor BDNF change (-3.9\u2009±\u20096.2 vs. 2.3\u2009±\u20096.7, P\u2009=\u20090.009). The increased percentage of BDNF was inversely correlated with the change in Zung SDS (r\u2009=\u2009-0.380, P\u2009=\u20090.022). Multivariate regression analysis showed that reduction in BDNF was independently associated with change in Zung SDS (95% confidence interval -0.315 to -0.052, P\u2009=\u20090.008)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Zung SDS only significantly improved in men with increased fasting BDNF levels after a lifestyle intervention."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "TRIAL REGISTRATION",
          "content": "(NCT01065753, ClinicalTrials.gov)."
        }
      ]},
